                                STARTING OUT

Notice the small blinking line on your screen.  It is called the CURSOR.
The cursor points to the place on the screen where your writing or editing
will occur.  So if you want to write some text at a particular place, or
make a change in text that already exists, your first task is to move the
cursor to the right place.

You have already met one of the keys that helps you move around -- the PgDn
key.  Here are a few other important keys for moving through text:

..LAYOUT 4  
   - The PgUp key is like PgDn, but moves you "up" to earlier text.

   - The arrow keys near PgUp and PgDn let you move in smaller steps.


..LAYOUT 2  
   =>  Try moving the cursor around using the arrow keys.  Press each one
       a few times.  Then press and hold down the Cursor-left arrow key.


..LAYOUT 1  
Notice what happens when you reach the top or bottom of the screen using
Cursor-up or Cursor-down arrow keys.


..LAYOUT 2  
   =>  Move the cursor to the bottom of the screen, using the Cursor-down
       arrow key.  When you reach the bottom, press the Cursor-down key a
       few more times.  Then press and hold down the Cursor-down key and
       watch the text.


..LAYOUT 1  
What you are seeing is new text that "scrolls" into view on the screen, as
if it were mounted on a long roll.  The Cursor-up key works in the same way,
allowing you to scroll upward to earlier text.


..page

                               MOVING AROUND

Here are some other keys that help you move around in this tutorial and in
your own documents:


..LAYOUT 2  
   =>  (Remember, read all of these instructions before you begin following
       them.)  Try moving ahead to the end of this tutorial using Ctrl-PgDn.
       Hold down the Ctrl key and then press PgDn.  Each time you do this,
       you are advanced to the top of the next page.

..LAYOUT 3  
       Then come back here using Ctrl-PgUp and PgUp.  Look for the title
