                          REFORMATTING PARAGRAPHS

Let's find out more about what makes a word processor more than just a
typewriter with a TV screen.  Use the sample paragraph below for practice.

..LAYOUT 2  

   =>  Scroll down, using the cursor-down key, until the sample paragraph
       below is at the top of your screen.


..LAYOUT 1  
"It is helpful to compare word processing functions to conventional office
activities.  Typing still requires a keyboard.  Retrieving and storing are
simply electronic ways of going to a file drawer, pulling out a folder and
working with pages of text, and then returning the folder to the drawer."


..LAYOUT 2  
   =>  Move your cursor to the "h" of "helpful" in the first line of the
       paragraph above.  Turn on Insert mode by pressing the Ins key once if
       necessary.  (Remember, the large cursor indicates that Insert is on.)
       Then type the word "sometimes " including the space.  You'll see the
       line break automatically when you push it past the right margin.

   =>  Now watch what happens when you move the cursor.  Press the
       Cursor-right key once and watch the paragraph reform.

..LAYOUT 3  
       (If the paragraph does not reform, press Ctrl-F1 to go to the
       Preference Menu, and press R, so Automatic Reformatting indicates
       Yes.  Press U to use the preferences you have now set.  Then try
       again.)


..LAYOUT 1  
A paragraph will automatically be reformatted any time you make a change
that affects line length.  The automatic reformatting occurs when you move
the cursor or start an editing function.  Automatic reformatting stops at
the end of the paragraph - which you mark by pressing Enter, remember? - or
it stops when it reaches a blank line.

Any time you need to reformat a paragraph manually, press the F8 function
key.


..page

                                 INDENTING

Here is our sample paragraph again:

"It is helpful to compare word processing functions to conventional office
activities.  Typing still requires a keyboard.  Retrieving and storing are
simply electronic ways of going to a file drawer, pulling out a folder and
working with pages of text, and then returning the folder to the drawer."


..LAYOUT 2  
   =>  Move your cursor to the beginning  "  at the start of the paragraph
       above.  Turn on Insert by pressing the Ins key once if necessary.
       Then press the Tab key once, and move the cursor to trigger automatic
       reformatting.

..LAYOUT 1  
You now have an indented first line on the paragraph, where previously the
first line was flush left.  We'll soon see how easy it is to have the first
line of all your paragraphs indented for you automatically.


